+# PR #638 follow-up: red-team of the shared `.eh_frame` reader
+
+**Upstream base:** `8624a5d069f9473adeefdaae26378f69681d8bb4` (`origin/main`, fetched again on 2026-09-27; PR #639). The `.eh_frame` fix began on `a89e685` and was rebased cleanly onto the current base. **Scope:** the newly extended shared ELF `.eh_frame` reader in `src/backend/linker_common/eh_frame.rs`; the x86-64, i686, ARM and RISC-V executable linkers all call its FDE counter/header writer. This is a correctness and input-hardening change, **not** a demonstrated generated-code speedup.
+
+## Reproducer and root cause
+
+A 32-byte input with a DWARF64 length escape (`ff ff ff ff`), an extended length of `u64::MAX`, and a nonzero CIE pointer at byte 12 made `count_eh_frame_fdes` return **1** on the upstream fastbuild although the purported FDE extends far beyond the input. `scan_eh_frame_records` and the header decoder independently computed `body + (length as usize)` without checking overflow; release arithmetic wrapped to an earlier offset. The new unit test `oversized_extended_record_is_not_an_fde` was **RED against upstream** (`left: 1, right: 0`), then GREEN after the change. Separately, unsigned/signed LEB decoders could accept a missing terminator, shift by >=64, and treat bytes in a later record as part of this record. FDE initial-location decoding likewise had only an input-buffer bound, not an **FDE-record** bound. A DWARF64 CIE was recognized by the counter but its `R` augmentation was ignored by the address decoder. A second independent red test, `invalid_cie_pointer_cannot_fabricate_a_header_entry`, showed that a damaged CIE pointer silently fell back to absolute addressing and placed a bogus function address into the header's binary-search table.
+
+## Changes and design rationale
+
+- `record_bounds`: one constant-space structural check shared by the counter, relocated FDE scanner and unrelocated GC scanner. Reject impossible lengths with `usize::try_from`, `checked_add`, per-record ID-width validation and a buffer bound; every accepted record advances strictly. Pass the already-read length through, avoiding redundant loads in the counting path. This is cheaper and less error-prone than independently patching three near-identical scanners.
+- Decode the CIE within its own validated boundaries, for both 32- and 64-bit DWARF record formats. Treat a truncated augmentation, misplaced `R` byte or unknown variable-length personality field as **invalid** rather than reading the next CIE. The CIE cache now distinguishes `Some(0)` (valid absolute encoding) from `None` (invalid pointer/CIE) and omits undecodable FDEs instead of inventing search-table entries. Use the version-1 one-byte return-address register rule; newer CIEs use ULEB. Do not change the decoded format of existing ordinary CIEs.
+- Bound both LEB readers to at most ten bytes and reject values outside their signed/unsigned 64-bit domains. Pass a slice ending at the **FDE**, not at the enclosing section, to `decode_eh_pointer`. A bad input is not proof of a codegen speedup; the gain is deterministic parsing and safe failure.
+- Tests: oversized record and invalid CIE pointer (both red/green), unsigned/signed LEB limits and truncation, valid DWARF64 CIE+FDE pcrel address on both ELF bitnesses, and a deterministic 2,048-case arbitrary-byte/extended-length corpus checking count/scan agreement, monotone in-bounds records and panic-free header builds for 32/64-bit targets. The legacy shared-reader tests and live x86-64/i686 unwinding test must remain green.
+
+## Evidence and limits
+
+- Rust 1.98.1, `scripts/build_lccc_fast.sh` (Rust `-O1`, `-j2`, warnings denied), **6 GiB active swap**: build green.
+- Current focused run: **14/14** `.eh_frame` tests and `tests/regression/check_eh_frame_unwind.sh`, including **i686** after installing multilib. CI history: first run **82 passed, 1 failed (rustfmt), 3 intentionally skipped**; after formatting, second run **83 passed, 0 failed, 3 skipped**, including 3,601 library tests (7 ignored), CFI invariants, both-mode unwinding, linker fuzz, GAS 2.47.20260726 differential and Clippy. The invalid-CIE fix was subsequently rebased onto PR #639 and the rebased **exact tree** passed fast CI independently: 83 passed, 0 failed, 3 skipped. Further changes (the separate I64 map optimization) need their own exact-tree CI stamp; do not treat the earlier stamp as validation of later edits. The *exact-tree* final verdict is captured by `target/ci_local.pass` and the `ci_gate` field of the external session ledger (`/home/user/artifacts/SNAPSHOT_LEDGER.md`); only a matching gated snapshot certifies delivery. A fast CI baseline *before* multilib/large swap failed due to the environment: the Rust lib-test compile was SIGKILLed with only 1 GiB swap, and a header from `libc6-dev-i386` was absent. Those prerequisites were installed; they are not silently counted as compiler fixes.
+- No target i7-14700KF, PMU or controlled Raptor Lake measurements exist here. No claim about GCC 16.2 / Clang 23.1 / ICX speed, all supported architecture runtimes, or general fuzz completeness is made. The system GAS is 2.44 and **was not used as an encoding oracle**; the CI assembler-differential gate provisions GAS 2.47 itself.
+
+## Next-agent queue (not completed in this session)
+
+1. Preserve a matching exact-tree `scripts/ci_local.sh --fast` stamp for **every delivered edit**; if a gate fails, retain its log and diagnose the defect rather than masking it. Run the full (slow) gates on an adequately provisioned host.
+2. Differentially exercise emitted `.eh_frame_hdr` entries against GNU ld 2.47, mold 2.42.1 and lld 23.1 using **well-formed and malformed** real objects for x86-64, i686, ARM and RISC-V. The native VM can run x86-64 and multilib i686; the other target runtimes need a cross-toolchain/QEMU. The mold 2.42.1 source confirms `-DMOLD_TARGETS='X86_64;I386'` in `tools/linker/setup_oracles.sh` is already the right narrowly-scoped CMake option; do not rename it speculatively. Download/build of all linker oracles and a complete glibc 2.44/kernel validation were not performed here.
+3. Remaining `eh_frame` policy/design question: this fix **skips** an FDE pointing to an invalid CIE. Decide whether malformed CIEs should make the **whole** input link fail instead; audit relocation handling and `.eh_frame_hdr` reservation, which counts structurally valid FDEs before decoding and can reserve more space than the table actually uses if an input CIE is invalid.
+4. Codegen is tracked separately in `FOLLOWUP-2026-09-27-map-i64-performance.md`. The GCC 16.2/Clang 23.1/ICC/ICX four-workload survey, four timed real-workload proxies, and a measured two-lane I64 map optimization are recorded there. VM timings are screening evidence, not hardware proof; outstanding Expat/SQLite gaps need further profiling before optimizing their hot paths.
+5. Investigate and measure other PR #638 areas (CFI state transfer through indirect branches; ARX latency; TCE side-effect screening; COMDAT signature handling), including malformed-input fuzzers, target-specific runtime unwinding and an independent linker differential. This focused follow-up is **not** a whole-codebase audit.
